VALUES (4600093823020129, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain and apply Importance of process calculations in “Introduction”; include the main principle, an example and a verification or limitation."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain and apply Definition of Atomic weight and Molecular wt. in “Basic Chemical Calculations”; include the main principle, an example and a verification or limitation."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain and apply Definition of Law of Conversation of Mass in “Material Balance In Process Without Chemical Reactions”; include the main principle, an example and a verification or limitation."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain and apply Definition: limiting Reactant in “Material Balance In Process Involving Chemical Reactions”; include the main principle, an example and a verification or limitation."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning (2):** Importance of process calculations is covered in “Introduction”. Define its role, describe the main steps/components, connect it to Dimensions and systems of units, Fundamental quantities, Derived quantities, Definition and units of force, give a practical example, and add a verification check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Describe the concept in sequence and connect it with Dimensions and systems of units, Fundamental quantities, Derived quantities, Definition and units of force.\n\n**Application/example (2):** Apply the concept to a practical INDUSTRIAL STOICHIOMETRY task and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result and mention one limitation or risk.\n\nMarking guide: meaning 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning (2):** Definition of Atomic weight and Molecular wt. is covered in “Basic Chemical Calculations”. Define its role, describe the main steps/components, connect it to Calculation of Molecular weight of various compounds, Introduction to Avogadro’s Number & Moles concept, relation between Mole and Atomic weight, Basic Mole calculations, give a practical example, and add a verification check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Describe the concept in sequence and connect it with Calculation of Molecular weight of various compounds, Introduction to Avogadro’s Number & Moles concept, relation between Mole and Atomic weight, Basic Mole calculations.\n\n**Application/example (2):** Apply the concept to a practical INDUSTRIAL STOICHIOMETRY task and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result and mention one limitation or risk.\n\nMarking guide: meaning 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning (2):** Definition of Law of Conversation of Mass is covered in “Material Balance In Process Without Chemical Reactions”. Define its role, describe the main steps/components, connect it to Process flow diagram, significance of Material balance in chemical industry, Simple M.B. problems on operations like Drying, Distillation, give a practical example, and add a verification check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Describe the concept in sequence and connect it with Process flow diagram, significance of Material balance in chemical industry, Simple M.B. problems on operations like Drying, Distillation.\n\n**Application/example (2):** Apply the concept to a practical INDUSTRIAL STOICHIOMETRY task and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result and mention one limitation or risk.\n\nMarking guide: meaning 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning (2):** A limiting reactant is consumed first and sets the theoretical maximum product for a reaction.\n\n**Principles/steps (3):** Describe the concept in sequence and connect it with Excess reactant, Simple problems for finding yield, conversion and composition, Simple calculation of material balance based on reaction..\n\n**Application/example (2):** Apply the concept to a practical INDUSTRIAL STOICHIOMETRY task and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1):** Check the result and mention one limitation or risk.\n\nMarking guide: meaning 2; principles 3; application 2; verification 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- MATHEMATICS-III | Mid 1 | medium | TestId 460091310302015 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (460091310302015, ARRAY['"[8 marks] A practical problem in Mathematics-III requires the behaviour or method described by order matters when counting arrangements of selected objects. Explain how Basic permutation applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Mathematics-III requires the behaviour or method described by for equally likely outcomes, favourable outcomes are divided by the total outcomes. Explain how Classical probability applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Mathematics-III requires the behaviour or method described by a power of a sum is expanded using binomial coefficients and powers of the terms. Explain how Binomial theorem (without proof) for positive integral index (expansion and general form applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] A practical problem in Mathematics-III requires the behaviour or method described by the syllabus area concerned with Definition. Explain how Definition applies, outline the main steps and justify the result."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** A permutation counts arrangements in which order matters; arranging r distinct objects selected from n gives n!/(n−r)!.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Basic combination; Permutation and Combination. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Mathematics-III task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** For equally likely outcomes, classical probability is favourable outcomes divided by all outcomes. The complement rule is P(not A)=1−P(A).\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Compliment of event; Probability. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Mathematics-III task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** The binomial theorem expands a power of a sum using binomial coefficients and the exponents of the terms.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Binomial theorem for any index (expansion without proof) first and second binomial approximation with applications to engineering problems; Binomial Theorem. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Mathematics-III task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb, '"Model answer (8 marks)\n\n**Meaning and purpose (2 marks):** Definition is covered in the syllabus unit “Differentiation”. A complete answer should define the concept, describe its role and steps/components, relate it to Some properties, Derivative of some important functions, Chain rule, Derivative of implicit function, provide a practical example, and state a relevant check or limitation.\n\n**Principles/steps (3 marks):** Explain the concept in logical order and connect it to syllabus points such as Some properties; Derivative of some important functions; Chain rule; Derivative of implicit function. State relevant rules, components or assumptions and how they affect the outcome.\n\n**Application/example (2 marks):** Apply it to a concrete Mathematics-III task. Identify the objective, explain the selected procedure, and state the expected result.\n\n**Verification/conclusion (1 mark):** Check the result against the objective; mention an important limitation, error source or trade-off. Conclude why the approach fits the task.\n\nMarking guide: definition/purpose 2; principles/steps 3; application/example 2; verification/conclusion 1."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

